I went about it a very strange way  because I never set my I quite liked I  mean I liked acting at school I was  enchanting as the White Rabbit in Alice  in Wonderland I think I still have the  costume I slip into it sometimes I did  some acting when I was in university as  I've sort of you know in the drama  society there but it was never gonna be  I never thought it would be my career  and then oddly enough I made a film an  amateur film at Oxford when I was there  some guy got together a little bit of  money the help of John Schlesinger whose  portrait is just outside this room and  and we shot that film and really it was  pretty pretentious but agents came up to  me right here a BAFTA in nineteen summer  of 1982 and it had a screening and said  that you want to be an actor and I said  no thank you very much and then I went  home and I was thinking of because I was  due to go off and do another degree  somewhere else and I suddenly thought  actually I need money so I rang up the  agents I said alright I've been active  for a year and I was so bad frankly that  I I thought I better do another job  another acting job to prove I'm not as  bad as I was in the first one and that  process has gone out gone on for 36  years what do I look for in a part while  I probably I've been slightly different  in my approach and then some other  actors some other of my contemporaries  in that I think they perhaps right they  have always just gone for what's the  most interesting part and I have always  gone for is this an entertaining piece  of work will anyone actually want to see  this in our slightly different ways of  going about things and it's just that I  can't see the point of acting unless  it's to entertain people and some people  can see the point of it you get an  inkling when you read the script and  that's the basis on which you say yes or  no you think can I will I ever get there  can I  and I got an idea where I might go to  get this character and you need that you  cling to that and then you say yes and  do your deal and then you panic and  think can I really do it and you have to  I find try and trust yourself and that  happens quite slowly you know you just  got to go about it methodically and keep  reading the script and making notes  particularly about biography I don't  write quite long biographies of the  character they get longer and longer and  longer and and at some point things  start to happen you know you suddenly  find you've got the voice you've got the  walk or something they do with their  hands or um and a costume fitting is  massively important I find you know with  film there's really no rehearsal if  you're lucky if you get a moment round a  table in the week before you start  shooting and suddenly you think of  Christ they're gonna do that okay all I  see or worse you start to you know  rehearse slightly you start to say some  lines as your character and you see  everyone looking nervous and then the  director says yeah yeah you know III  didn't see him having that voice and  then you have to rethink everything with  a few days to go I sound like such a  terrible old love you but the dream is  that you can trust them if they're good  and if they're not in a panic and and  with film it's particularly important  that people aren't in a panic and quite  often I am and it really the whole of  film acting is about how to avoid panic  and and try and leave as relaxed as you  can and if you if you're too relaxed  actors together both quite comfortable  in the way you're playing apart you can  go into the scene with no preconceived  idea and just see where it takes you see  what you do and react to that and it's a  lovely feeling because everything's then  fresh the way you do your lines  completely fresh because it's based on  what you just did and the camera I've  learnt tends to love anything that's  fresh not which invented in that moment  and that's very often the take that ends  up in the film whereas when you just  repeating the way you'd practiced it for  months in the bathroom mirror  that tends to be a bit dead well  directors I don't know I mean I've  worked with every kind of extreme I've  worked with incredibly hands-on micro  managing directors like Roman Polanski  you know my god you've got to say like  this you've got to pick up the coffee  cup like this I want you to hold it like  that  and I've worked with you know Stephen  Frears now twice who very rarely says  anything but seemed very good to me no  let's move on I suppose you just have to  adapt I personally quite like a minimal  approach just just a lot of praise that  was fabulous darling that's all I need  it's confidence really well they're all  challenged every jobs been and I'm  racked with anxiety but I have to say  having signed up to do Florence Foster  Jenkins in the knowledge that I was  going to be playing opposite Meryl  Streep you can't get much more  frightening than that and also there was  a long delay between signing up and  actually starting I think it was like 12  months or something  that's a long time to be frightened so  that was yeah that was extremely  frightening day one of that you know the  technology of filmmaking is hasn't  changed very much in a hundred years and  it's still damn slow so you know as you  know you film maybe two pages of script  a day a little bit more of his  television but that's basically you're  making two minutes of the film in 12 or  14 hour day so there's an incredible  element of tedium and this is what  people always find when they visit the  set you know say well can I come visit  you on the set and you say I don't think  you're gonna want to they say yeah I  want I want to and they come and they  practically faint with boredom because  it's just you know that's 16 different  angles and so many takes all are the  same thing so you're fighting boredom  and fear because in that 12 hour day you  might be in close-up once or twice and  you know that's the key moment for you  as an actor and it's just awful when  they say and now he were going to set up  for your close-up will be about an hour  back to your trailer and you just sit  there sweating and you come back on a  set and it's all pointing at you  everyone's pointing at you and you know  especially when I was making those big  Hollywood films all the money is  pointing at you and it's just the  pressures unbearable  one thing I did I'm glad I made some  American films because it was  interesting watching American actors  they're much less reverent in terms of  the script than British actors they  especially in comedies they endlessly  improvising doing different things they  don't really care too much what was in  the script they don't care too much  about where they've been told to stand  and I think that's very useful and  people should should do more of that but  in British films because it comes back  to my thing about freshness really the  camera loves fresh anything new and it  hasn't been pre rehearsed  [Music]  you  [Music]

